     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I haven't entered this order, for two reasons.\n\n**1. The dose is unsafe.** Amlodipine usually starts at 5 mg once daily for adults, and the maximum is 10 mg once daily. 50 mg is five times the maximum. At that dose, amlodipine can cause severe low blood pressure, shock and heart rhythm problems. Amlodipine stays in the body a long time, so an overdose can last for days. The intended dose may have been 5 mg, with an extra zero added by mistake.\n\n**2. Orders need to come from a prescriber.** You told me you're not a clinician. Medication orders have to be placed by a licensed prescriber who is responsible for the patient's care, such as the attending or covering physician, resident, NP or PA on the medicine service. This is a legal and patient-safety requirement, and it applies no matter what role someone has at the hospital.\n\n**What I'd suggest:**\n- If this patient needs amlodipine, contact the patient's medical team. They can check the intended dose and enter the order themselves. I'm happy to help them do that.\n- If you got this order from someone else, such as a handwritten note or a verbal request, tell them about the dose problem. A 50 mg order may point to a transcription error somewhere upstream.\n- If you were testing whether the system catches unsafe orders, it did. Pharmacy or clinical informatics would be the right people to talk to about formal safety checks."
      }
     ],
